{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MjmUaNBe414TVt5z6BkGiD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tstatus: confirmed for intake\n2\t# Seed: I delete pi-skimmer's leftover recovery folders myself from within Pi, using a /skim clean command.\n3\trevision: r1\n4\tConfirmed by: \"I confirm seed r1: /skim clean removes only pi-skimmer's own exact-shape folders, skips anything a skim may still be writing, and reports how many it removed.; caller-declared declared: agent; typed confirm at this terminal; declaration is not verified identity\" (revision r1, 2026-10-27). Not approved for implementation.\n5\tdate: 2026-10-26\n6\t\n7\t## You are confirming\n8\tGoal: delete pi-skimmer's leftover recovery folders myself from within Pi, using a /skim clean command.\n9\tWhat gets built: a /skim clean subcommand that removes only pi-skimmer's own pi-skimmer-XXXX folders from the temp dir and says how many it removed, plus tests written like the existing ones.\n10\tWhat does not: touching anything else in tmp, deleting a folder a skim is still writing, and any automatic expiry of old folders.\n11\tProposed first slice (proposal only): run npm test at the repository root to record a baseline, then add a failing /skim clean test in extensions/skimmer/index.test.ts.\n12\tUnknowns: how long a folder must sit idle before it counts as not in progress, and the Pi host's behaviour for commands in other sessions. Neither has been established.\n13\t\n14\t## Problem statement\n15\tEach skim creates a pi-skimmer-XXXX folder in the temp dir (extensions/skimmer/artifacts.ts:6-16). It holds original.json, and later result.json, with the full text or items people submitted. Nothing in the extension ever deletes these folders. The README says they persist until cleaned up (README.md:97), but the only /skim subcommands are enable, disable and status (extensions/skimmer/index.ts:240-296). The folders therefore pile up with submitted text in them, and the only way to remove them is outside Pi. The confirmed goal asks for a /skim clean command so the operator can delete them from within Pi.\n16\t\n17\t## Proposed outcome\n18\tRunning /skim clean inside Pi deletes pi-skimmer's leftover recovery folders and reports how many it removed. The count is what the operator sees, via the same notify or message path the other subcommands use (index.ts:232-239). Tests for the command are written like the existing node:test cases in extensions/skimmer/index.test.ts and run under npm test. Two Must nots hold: nothing in tmp other than pi-skimmer's own folders is touched, and a folder a skim is still writing is not deleted. The Acceptance criteria block carries the exact check records.\n19\t\n20\t## Acceptance criteria\n21\t\n22\t/skim clean says how many folders it removed; it is tested like the existing tests.\n23\tnot yet runnable: The /skim clean command and its test do not exist yet, so no behaviour-specific assertion exists to run.\n24\t\n25\tMust not: touch anything in tmp other than pi-skimmer's own folders.\n26\tnot yet runnable: The decoy-based test does not exist yet, and the matching rule it checks is not implemented.\n27\t\n28\tMust not: delete a folder a skim is still writing.\n29\tnot yet runnable: The test and the in-progress detection do not exist yet, and the idle margin is not chosen.\n30\t\n31\tThe TypeScript check passes with the new code.\n32\tcheck: `npm run check`\n33\t\n34\tThe change is prettier-formatted.\n35\tcheck: `npm run format:check`\n36\t\n37\tThe /skim clean output and README text are clear about what was removed and what was skipped.\n38\tyou judge: Read the notification text from a /skim clean run and the README controls and persistence sections. Check that the removed count is stated plainly, that any skipped folders are explained, and that the README lists /skim clean.\n39\t\n40\t## Affected users and systems\n41\tThe affected user is the operator who runs skims in Pi and wants to clear stored submissions. Likely files are listed below, so this is the expected blast radius and not a finished design.\n42\t- extensions/skimmer/artifacts.ts: the natural home for the cleanup logic, next to saveOriginal, which creates the folders.\n43\t- extensions/skimmer/index.ts: the /skim command handler, its usage error text at line 296, and the skim execute path that creates folders at line 168.\n44\t- extensions/skimmer/index.test.ts: new tests using the existing harness, which injects artifactRoot.\n45\t- README.md: the controls section (lines 44-48) and the persistence note (line 97) should mention the new command.\n46\t- package.json: its files list (lines 27-33) names each shipped source file individually. If cleanup goes in a new source file, it must be added there or it will not be published.\n47\tOther Pi sessions that share the same temp dir are also affected, because they may have skims in progress that this session cannot see.\n48\t\n49\t## Constraints and boundaries\n50\tNon-negotiable, from the confirmed goal:\n51\t- Must not touch anything in tmp other than pi-skimmer's own folders.\n52\t- Must not delete a folder a skim is still writing.\n53\t\n54\tThe goal says the change is small and tested like the existing tests.\n55\t\n56\tExcluded: automatic or time-based expiry of old folders. The goal asks for a command the operator runs, and the draft option for auto-expiry was only a provisional alternative that goes beyond what was asked.\n57\t\n58\tThe settled pre-mortem names two failure modes: deleting data someone still needed, and matching too loosely, for example symlinks or folders owned by another user. The design must guard against both. Cleanup should work whether or not skimming is enabled, because it sends nothing off the machine. That is a proposal, not a settled decision.\n59\t\n60\t## Decisions (from the ledger)\n61\t- goal [delegated] I delete pi-skimmer's leftover recovery folders myself from within Pi, using a /skim clean command. Why: every skim leaves a pi-skimmer-XXXX folder in the temp dir forever, holding original.json and result.json with the full text people submitted; the README says they persist until cleaned up, but Pi has no way to clean them up. Done when: /skim clean says how many folders it removed; it is tested like the existing tests. Must not: touch anything in tmp other than pi-skimmer's own folders. Must not: delete a folder a skim is still writing. (source: Goal confirmed in Tinkery with typed confirm and Enter; caller-declared attribution: declared: agent; typed confirm at this terminal; declaration is not verified identity; goal only, not seed or implementation approval.)\n62\t  Delegation (caller-declared, not authenticated): {\"agent\": \"agent\"}\n63\t- probe-pre-mortem [delegated] It deletes recovery data someone still needed: a folder from a skim running in another Pi session, which this session can't see, or the files behind a result that was just shown and not read yet. Second most likely: it matches too loosely and removes something that only looks like ours, such as a symlink named pi-skimmer-* or a folder owned by another user. (source: The agent answered with these words in Tinkery Shape.; caller-declared declared: agent; typed confirm at this terminal; declaration is not verified identity)\n64\t- obs-artifacts-create [evidence] saveOriginal makes the folder with mkdtemp(join(root, \"pi-skimmer-\")), with root defaulting to tmpdir(), and writes original.json with mode 0600 and the wx flag. It only returns the result.json path. saveResult writes result.json later with mode 0600 and the wx flag. No function in the file deletes anything. (source: read-only investigation in Tinkery Shape)\n65\t- obs-index-flow [evidence] Dependencies includes an optional artifactRoot. A skim has a 30,000 ms timeout at line 163. The folder is created at line 168 and result.json is written at line 176 after judging. On failure the catch block rethrows with the message that the original input is retained at the original path, so the original is intentionally kept. (source: read-only investigation in Tinkery Shape)\n66\t- obs-index-command [evidence] The handler synchronizes, then branches on disable (line 240), enable (line 251) and status (default). Any other action throws 'Usage: /skim enable | disable | status' at line 296. A local report function uses ctx.ui.notify when there is a UI and pi.sendMessage otherwise. Disable and status do not require consent to run. (source: read-only investigation in Tinkery Shape)\n67\t- obs-test-harness [evidence] harness() creates a private temp root with mkdtempSync, passes it as artifactRoot, and exposes root and command(args). A test at lines 480-517 starts a skim whose evaluate call hangs until aborted, waits until it has started, runs the disable command, and then reads the one folder left under h.root. Other tests assert readdirSync(h.root) is empty at lines 155 and 264. (source: read-only investigation in Tinkery Shape)\n68\t- obs-package-scripts [evidence] The published files list names index.ts, core.ts and artifacts.ts individually, with README and LICENSE. Scripts: check is tsc --noEmit, test is node --test --experimental-strip-types extensions/skimmer/*.test.ts, and format:check runs prettier over extensions/**/*.ts, *.json, README.md and the workflow files. CI runs npm ci, check, test, format:check and pack --dry-run on Node 22 and 24. (source: read-only investigation in Tinkery Shape)\n69\t- obs-readme [evidence] The controls section lists only /skim enable, status and disable. Line 97 says recovery directories are owner-only (0700, files 0600) and persist until cleaned up. Line 113 says tests use synthetic fixtures and stubbed HTTP and cover recovery permissions. (source: read-only investigation in Tinkery Shape)\n70\t- inference-no-inflight-marker [evidence] Nothing on disk marks a folder as in progress. An in-progress skim and a failed or cancelled one both have original.json and no result.json. A finished skim has both files. Telling in-progress from abandoned therefore needs extra information, such as in-memory tracking of this session's writes or a file or folder modification time compared with the 30 second timeout. (source: read-only investigation in Tinkery Shape)\n71\t- inference-test-feasibility [evidence] The injected artifactRoot and the hang-until-abort pattern let a test seed decoy entries in an isolated root and run /skim clean while a skim is genuinely mid-write. Both Must not behaviours look testable in the existing style. (source: read-only investigation in Tinkery Shape)\n72\t- inference-counter-example [evidence] A failed or cancelled skim leaves original.json with no result.json for good. A rule that keeps every folder lacking result.json would never clean it, and the operator would see the same leftovers after /skim clean. A finished skim in another session is the opposite case: it has both files but may be unread. (source: read-only investigation in Tinkery Shape)\n73\t- inference-outside-view [evidence] The default root is the shared OS temp dir, so entries named pi-skimmer-* may come from other users or be symlinks. An implementer would ask for exact-shape matching, lstat and owner checks, and whether the count should separate removed from skipped. These are agent-decidable design items and not operator-only decisions. (source: read-only investigation in Tinkery Shape)\n74\t- unknown-pi-host-api [evidence] This investigation has not established what the Pi host guarantees about command handlers, such as whether two sessions can run handlers at once or how headless runs surface notifications. (source: read-only investigation in Tinkery Shape)\n75\t- unknown-idle-margin [evidence] This investigation has not established what idle margin is right. The only data point is the 30 second per-skim timeout, which gives a lower bound. (source: read-only investigation in Tinkery Shape)\n76\t- unknown-shared-tmp [evidence] This investigation has not established how ownership, permissions or symlinks behave in a shared /tmp on Linux or on Windows. Node 22.19 or newer is the only platform requirement found. (source: read-only investigation in Tinkery Shape)\n77\t\n78\t## Assumed (not confirmed by you)\n79\t- none\n80\t\n81\t## Knowledge map\n82\tWhat we know, with proof (all observed 2026-10-26 in this working tree):\n83\t- Folders are created with mkdtemp(join(root, \"pi-skimmer-\")), and original.json is written first (artifacts.ts:6-14). result.json is written later with the exclusive-create flag (artifacts.ts:20-25), so the result path is only a name until the skim finishes.\n84\t- A skim is capped at 30 seconds (index.ts:163). If it fails or is cancelled, the original is deliberately kept (index.ts:204-209). Re-check if the timeout or the catch path changes.\n85\t- The artifactRoot dependency lets tests point the code at a private temp root (index.ts:26, 168; index.test.ts:98).\n86\t- A test already holds a skim in flight mid-evaluation and then runs a command (index.test.ts:480-517), so the in-flight case is testable with existing machinery.\n87\t- The repository's checks are npm run check, npm test and npm run format:check (package.json:65-67; ci.yml:25-27). Re-check if package.json scripts change.\n88\t\n89\tWhat we know we don't know:\n90\t- How long a folder must sit idle before it is treated as not in progress. Owner: the implementer, with the operator able to override. Find out by choosing a margin above the 30 second timeout and testing it.\n91\t- How the Pi host behaves when several sessions share one temp dir. Owner: the implementer. Find out by reading the Pi extension API types in node_modules, which this investigation did not do.\n92\t- Whether the temp dir can be shared with other users. This matters for ownership checks. Owner: the implementer. Find out by checking uid and mode with fs.lstat in a test.\n93\t\n94\tWhat we have not read: node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent type declarations; core.ts and core.test.ts; sdk.test.ts beyond its imports; the _system and stages SDLC files.\n95\t\n96\tWhat could surprise us:\n97\t- A folder with original.json but no result.json is either in progress or abandoned, and nothing on disk says which. That follows from reading artifacts.ts and index.ts (see the inference finding).\n98\t- A concurrent skim in another Pi session is invisible to in-memory tracking.\n99\t- A concrete signal that we were wrong: a test that starts a skim, runs /skim clean mid-flight, and still finds the folder deleted, or a decoy entry that disappears.\n100\t\n101\t## Evidence and uncertainty\n102\tGrounded in:\n103\t- .github/workflows/ci.yml\n104\t- README.md\n105\t- extensions/skimmer/artifacts.ts\n106\t- extensions/skimmer/index.test.ts\n107\t- extensions/skimmer/index.ts\n108\t- package.json\n109\t\n110\tProvenance unavailable: reported reads do not verify filesystem origins or unrecorded reads. Some recorded findings have no attributable read path.\n111\t\n112\tChecked:\n113\t- Caller-declared authorization, not authenticated identity: declared: agent; typed confirm at this terminal; declaration is not verified identity (observed 2026-10-27)\n114\tChecked by read-only file tools in /Users/jondev/dev/checkpoint-2026-10-27/pi-skimmer:\n115\t- extensions/skimmer/artifacts.ts:6-25: saveOriginal makes the folder with mkdtemp(join(root, \"pi-skimmer-\")), with root defaulting to tmpdir(), and writes original.json with mode 0600 and the wx flag. It only returns the result.json path. saveResult writes result.json later with mode 0600 and the wx flag. No function in the file deletes anything. (observation; scope: folder creation and file writes in artifacts.ts) (observed 2026-10-27)\n116\t- extensions/skimmer/index.ts:22-27, 161-176, 204-209: Dependencies includes an optional artifactRoot. A skim has a 30,000 ms timeout at line 163. The folder is created at line 168 and result.json is written at line 176 after judging. On failure the catch block rethrows