{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS8yjwETEHyzgmvPih7J","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XgEWT3Z3qywUZfJEQZifCg","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/pi-skimmer/i-delete-pi-skimmer-s-leftover-recovery-folders-myself-from-within-pi-using-a-sk/README.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17164,"cache_read_input_tokens":25212,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17164},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f309f9c8-1473-4f76-8d79-3d58dcb79201","uuid":"23a2c3b8-161f-445d-9c37-afd2d3eabeff","timestamp":"2026-10-27T04:49:32.910Z","request_id":"req_011CgS8yjg6zRmVrNsWyRztY"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XgEWT3Z3qywUZfJEQZifCg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# pi-skimmer\n2\t\n3\tGoal-directed filtering for the [Pi coding agent](https://pi.dev), powered by Jev through [pi-typesafe](https://github.com/DevMortimer/pi-typesafe).\n4\t\n5\t- `skim_text`: retain relevant chunks of supplied text, with line ranges.\n6\t- `filter_items`: retain relevant JSON records, optionally showing Jev only selected fields.\n7\t- Explicit consent, bounded requests, owner-only recovery files, and preservation of unjudged material.\n8\t\n9\tThis is a native Pi extension, not a wrapper around the Skimmer CLI. It does not intercept reads, fetch URLs, open source files, or expand paths.\n10\t\n11\t## Install\n12\t\n13\tRequires Pi 1.2.0 or newer and Node.js 22.19 or newer.\n14\t\n15\tInstall directly from GitHub:\n16\t\n17\t```sh\n18\tpi install git:github.com/jon-devlapaz/pi-skimmer\n19\t```\n20\t\n21\tAfter the npm release is published, you can also use:\n22\t\n23\t```sh\n24\tpi install npm:pi-skimmer\n25\t```\n26\t\n27\tThe TypeSafe SDK is installed as a dependency. To use the interactive `/typesafe login` and `/typesafe status` commands, also install its extension:\n28\t\n29\t```sh\n30\tpi install npm:pi-typesafe@0.9.1\n31\t```\n32\t\n33\tRestart Pi or run `/reload`, then:\n34\t\n35\t```text\n36\t/typesafe login\n37\t/skim enable\n38\t```\n39\t\n40\tAlternatively, configure `TYPESAFE_API_KEY` in your own environment before starting Pi. Never paste credentials into tool arguments or commit them.\n41\t\n42\t## Consent and controls\n43\t\n44\t```text\n45\t/skim enable\n46\t/skim status\n47\t/skim disable\n48\t```\n49\t\n50\tConsent is separate from `/typesafe enable`, scoped to the current Pi session and working directory, and revoked on reload. The confirmation identifies the destination host. Disabling cancels active skims and blocks future ones.\n51\t\n52\tFor headless use, the operator must explicitly set `PI_SKIMMER_ENABLED=1` before starting Pi. Do not have an agent set this as a workaround for missing consent.\n53\t\n54\tThe SDK client allows 100 attempted requests per extension session and sets a shared estimated daily spending cap of $1. Lower `PI_TYPESAFE_MAX_*` environment caps still apply. Estimates are not billing guarantees. The default request timeout is 10 seconds, with an overall tool timeout of 30 seconds.\n55\t\n56\t## Tools\n57\t\n58\t### `skim_text`\n59\t\n60\tSupply already obtained, approved text and a focused goal:\n61\t\n62\t```json\n63\t{\n64\t  \"text\": \"Retry policy: allow at most four attempts. Cancellation interrupts retries immediately.\",\n65\t  \"goal\": \"Find retry limits and cancellation requirements\",\n66\t  \"source\": \"supplied retry policy\"\n67\t}\n68\t```\n69\t\n70\tDefaults are 2,000 Unicode characters per chunk, 10% overlap, and a relevance threshold of `0.5`. Optional controls: `chunk_chars` (500–8,000), `overlap` (0–0.5), and `threshold` (0–1).\n71\t\n72\tLine ranges are relative to the supplied text, not an independently read source. `source` is a display label; it is sent to Jev but never fetched or opened.\n73\t\n74\t### `filter_items`\n75\t\n76\t```json\n77\t{\n78\t  \"items\": [\n79\t    { \"title\": \"Authentication bypass\", \"tracking_id\": \"SEC-1\" },\n80\t    { \"title\": \"Change button color\", \"tracking_id\": \"UI-2\" }\n81\t  ],\n82\t  \"fields\": [\"title\"],\n83\t  \"goal\": \"Keep exploitable security vulnerabilities\"\n84\t}\n85\t```\n86\t\n87\tAccepts 1–256 JSON items. `fields` selects literal top-level fields, not dotted paths. Every requested field must exist on every item; otherwise the entire call fails before transmission. There is no fallback to the full record.\n88\t\n89\t**Field selection is not output redaction.** Jev receives only selected fields, but retained records and local originals preserve all fields. Returned records can be visible to your coding model. Do not include credentials or other unapproved data, even in unselected fields.\n90\t\n91\t## Privacy and failure behavior\n92\t\n93\t- Only submit approved, nonsensitive data. Text or selected fields, the goal, and the source label leave the machine. Requests may incur charges.\n94\t- Obvious credential patterns are rejected; this is not comprehensive secret detection.\n95\t- Input over 256,000 UTF-8 JSON bytes fails instead of silently truncating. Large individual records must be split explicitly.\n96\t- Unjudged items are retained with warnings. Degraded API outcomes are explicitly marked as errors, not successful relevance judgments.\n97\t- Full input and results are saved in temporary, owner-only recovery directories (`0700`, files `0600`). They contain the original data and persist until cleaned up; treat them accordingly. Temporary files are not permanent archival storage.\n98\t- Displayed output is capped, with explicit truncation metadata and paths to complete recovery files. There is no top-N cap discarding retained material.\n99\t- Relevance scores are probabilistic. They are not proof, authorization, or exhaustive verification. Read originals before critical decisions, especially for cross-references, constraints, and exceptions.\n100\t\n101\tThe most useful pattern is to obtain approved text or records through existing tools, then filter them in a tool script before displaying the large raw result to the coding model. Calling `skim_text` after a large document is already in model context does not reclaim those earlier tokens.\n102\t\n103\t## Development\n104\t\n105\t```sh\n106\tnpm ci --ignore-scripts\n107\tnpm run check\n108\tnpm test\n109\tnpm run format:check\n110\tnpm pack --dry-run --ignore-scripts\n111\t```\n112\t\n113\tTests use synthetic fixtures and stubbed HTTP responses; they do not require a Jev key or make paid inference requests. They cover consent, real SDK command integration, strict projection, partial failure, budgets, cancellation, recovery permissions, and display caps.\n114\t\n115\tThe development-only npm overrides keep pi-typesafe's older optional Pi peer ranges aligned with the tested Pi SDK. Pi supplies the host packages at runtime; they are not bundled.\n116\t\n117\t## License\n118\t\n119\tMIT. See [LICENSE](LICENSE).\n120\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f309f9c8-1473-4f76-8d79-3d58dcb79201","uuid":"d2dfc99b-4951-461d-9d44-67704a413480","timestamp":"2026-10-27T04:49:32.921Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/pi-skimmer/i-delete-pi-skimmer-s-leftover-recovery-folders-myself-from-within-pi-using-a-sk/README.md","content":"# pi-skimmer\n\nGoal-directed filtering for the [Pi coding agent](https://pi.dev), powered by Jev through [pi-typesafe](https://github.com/DevMortimer/pi-typesafe).\n\n- `skim_text`: retain relevant chunks of supplied text, with line ranges.\n- `filter_items`: retain relevant JSON records, optionally showing Jev only selected fields.\n- Explicit consent, bounded requests, owner-only recovery files, and preservation of unjudged material.\n\nThis is a native Pi extension, not a wrapper around the Skimmer CLI. It does not intercept reads, fetch URLs, open source files, or expand paths.\n\n## Install\n\nRequires Pi 1.2.0 or newer and Node.js 22.19 or newer.\n\nInstall directly from GitHub:\n\n```sh\npi install git:github.com/jon-devlapaz/pi-skimmer\n```\n\nAfter the npm release is published, you can also use:\n\n```sh\npi install npm:pi-skimmer\n```\n\nThe TypeSafe SDK is installed as a dependency. To use the interactive `/typesafe login` and `/typesafe status` commands, also install its extension:\n\n```sh\npi install npm:pi-typesafe@0.9.1\n```\n\nRestart Pi or run `/reload`, then:\n\n```text\n/typesafe login\n/skim enable\n```\n\nAlternatively, configure `TYPESAFE_API_KEY` in your own environment before starting Pi. Never paste credentials into tool arguments or commit them.\n\n## Consent and controls\n\n```text\n/skim enable\n/skim status\n/skim disable\n```\n\nConsent is separate from `/typesafe enable`, scoped to the current Pi session and working directory, and revoked on reload. The confirmation identifies the destination host. Disabling cancels active skims and blocks future ones.\n\nFor headless use, the operator must explicitly set `PI_SKIMMER_ENABLED=1` before starting Pi. Do not have an agent set this as a workaround for missing consent.\n\nThe SDK client allows 100 attempted requests per extension session and sets a shared estimated daily spending cap of $1. Lower `PI_TYPESAFE_MAX_*` environment caps still apply. Estimates are not billing guarantees. The default request timeout is 10 seconds, with an overall tool timeout of 30 seconds.\n\n## Tools\n\n### `skim_text`\n\nSupply already obtained, approved text and a focused goal:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"text\": \"Retry policy: allow at most four attempts. Cancellation interrupts retries immediately.\",\n  \"goal\": \"Find retry limits and cancellation requirements\",\n  \"source\": \"supplied retry policy\"\n}\n```\n\nDefaults are 2,000 Unicode characters per chunk, 10% overlap, and a relevance threshold of `0.5`. Optional controls: `chunk_chars` (500–8,000), `overlap` (0–0.5), and `threshold` (0–1).\n\nLine ranges are relative to the supplied text, not an independently read source. `source` is a display label; it is sent to Jev but never fetched or opened.\n\n### `filter_items`\n\n```json\n{\n  \"items\": [\n    { \"title\": \"Authentication bypass\", \"tracking_id\": \"SEC-1\" },\n    { \"title\": \"Change button color\", \"tracking_id\": \"UI-2\" }\n  ],\n  \"fields\": [\"title\"],\n  \"goal\": \"Keep exploitable security vulnerabilities\"\n}\n```\n\nAccepts 1–256 JSON items. `fields` selects literal top-level fields, not dotted paths. Every requested field must exist on every item; otherwise the entire call fails before transmission. There is no fallback to the full record.\n\n**Field selection is not output redaction.** Jev receives only selected fields, but retained records and local originals preserve all fields. Returned records can be visible to your coding model. Do not include credentials or other unapproved data, even in unselected fields.\n\n## Privacy and failure behavior\n\n- Only submit approved, nonsensitive data. Text or selected fields, the goal, and the source label leave the machine. Requests may incur charges.\n- Obvious credential patterns are rejected; this is not comprehensive secret detection.\n- Input over 256,000 UTF-8 JSON bytes fails instead of silently truncating. Large individual records must be split explicitly.\n- Unjudged items are retained with warnings. Degraded API outcomes are explicitly marked as errors, not successful relevance judgments.\n- Full input and results are saved in temporary, owner-only recovery directories (`0700`, files `0600`). They contain the original data and persist until cleaned up; treat them accordingly. Temporary files are not permanent archival storage.\n- Displayed output is capped, with explicit truncation metadata and paths to complete recovery files. There is no top-N cap discarding retained material.\n- Relevance scores are probabilistic. They are not proof, authorization, or exhaustive verification. Read originals before critical decisions, especially for cross-references, constraints, and exceptions.\n\nThe most useful pattern is to obtain approved text or records through existing tools, then filter them in a tool script before displaying the large raw result to the coding model. Calling `skim_text` after a large document is already in model context does not reclaim those earlier tokens.\n\n## Development\n\n```sh\nnpm ci --ignore-scripts\nnpm run check\nnpm test\nnpm run format:check\nnpm pack --dry-run --ignore-scripts\n```\n\nTests use synthetic fixtures and stubbed HTTP responses; they do not require a Jev key or make paid inference requests. They cover consent, real SDK command integration, strict projection, partial failure, budgets, cancellation, recovery permissions, and display caps.\n\nThe development-only npm overrides keep pi-typesafe's older optional Pi peer ranges aligned with the tested Pi SDK. Pi supplies the host packages at runtime; they are not bundled.\n\n## License\n\nMIT. See [LICENSE](LICENSE).\n","numLines":120,"startLine":1,"totalLines":120}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XgEWT3Z3qywUZfJEQZifCg","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
