the checks. I marked nothing. All three behaviour items (`seed-1`, `seed-2`, `seed-3`) are written but unproven.\n\n## Brief\n\n`brief.md` is the unfilled template. This is a seed-bound run, so I worked from `seed-contract.md` and `seed-checks.json`.\n\n## What changed\n\n- `extensions/skimmer/artifacts.ts`\n  - `IDLE_MS` is 5 minutes. That is above the 30 s skim timeout, and it also gives some protection to a result that was just shown and not yet read.\n  - `cleanArtifacts(root, { active, idleMs })` returns counts of removed, recent, unrecognised and failed folders.\n  - `describeClean(report)` builds the operator message.\n- `extensions/skimmer/index.ts`\n  - New `clean` branch in the `/skim` handler. It needs no consent and works with or without a UI, through the existing `report` path.\n  - A `writing` set tracks the folder of each skim in progress. It is added after `saveOriginal` and removed in a new `finally`.\n  - The usage error now reads `Usage: /skim enable | disable | status | clean`.\n  - The command description mentions cleanup.\n- `extensions/skimmer/index.test.ts`: 6 new tests, listed below.\n- `README.md`: `/skim clean` in the controls block, a persistence bullet describing what is removed and skipped, and \"recovery cleanup\" in the test coverage line.\n- `package.json` is unchanged. I reused `artifacts.ts`, which is already in the published `files` list.\n\n## Matching rules\n\nA folder is deleted only if all of these hold:\n\n- Its name matches `^pi-skimmer-[A-Za-z0-9]{6}$` exactly, which is the `mkdtemp` shape. Names that don't match, like `pi-skimmer-notes`, are ignored and not counted.\n- `lstat` says it is a real directory, so symlinks are never followed.\n- Its owner is the current uid. Where `process.getuid` is absent (Windows), the owner check is skipped.\n- It contains only `original.json` and `result.json`, each a regular file with the same owner. Anything else makes it unrecognised and skipped.\n- No skim in this session is writing it, and nothing in it was modified within `IDLE_MS`.\n\nRemoval unlinks the two known files and then calls `rmdir`, not a recursive remove. A file added mid-delete makes `rmdir` fail, which counts as \"could not be removed\".\n\n## Output\n\nExamples of the notification text:\n\n- `Removed 2 recovery folders.`\n- `Removed 1 recovery folder. Skipped 3: 3 not a plain pi-skimmer folder (symlink, other owner, or unexpected contents).`\n- `Removed 0 recovery folders. Skipped 1: 1 in progress or modified within the last 5 minutes (run /skim clean again later).`\n\n## Tests added\n\nEach is written to the harness's `artifactRoot` and uses `utimesSync` to age folders.\n\n1. Removes abandoned and finished folders, with the count and the singular/plural/zero wording.\n2. Works headless and without consent: no client is built and no request is made.\n3. Decoys survive: a plain `pi-skimmer-AAAAAA` file, a symlink `pi-skimmer-BBBBBB` to an outside directory (its contents are checked), `pi-skimmer-notes`, an unrelated directory, and a shape-matching folder with an extra `notes.txt`. Only the genuine folder is removed.\n4. Folders modified inside the margin are skipped (age 0 and `IDLE_MS` minus 1 minute). One at `IDLE_MS` plus 1 minute is removed.\n5. A skim that hangs mid-evaluation keeps its folder through `/skim clean`, both fresh and backdated, so only the in-progress tracking can be protecting it in the backdated case. After `disable` aborts the skim, the aged folder is removed.\n6. An unknown subcommand's usage error lists `clean`.\n\nThe intended red (the usage error at the old `index.ts:296`) was reasoned from the code. I could not run it.\n\n## Not tested or not established\n\n- Other-user ownership has no test, because it needs a second uid. It is code-reviewed only.\n- Behaviour on Windows and on a shared Linux `/tmp` is not exercised.\n- I did not read the Pi host type declarations in `node_modules` (not installed), so cross-session handler behaviour and headless notification surfacing are still unknowns. Skims in other Pi sessions are covered only by the 5-minute age margin. The in-memory `writing` set cannot see them.\n- A small time-of-check/time-of-use gap remains between `lstat` and `unlink`. Owner-only checks narrow it, but it is not eliminated.\n- The unread-result risk is reduced, not removed. The README says so.\n- Judge item `seed-6` (clear removed/skipped wording, README lists `/skim clean`) is for the reviewer. I wrote the text but did not run the command to see it.\n\n## Skills\n\n`tink mount` is a shell command, so I couldn't read the `principle-build-the-lever` and `unslop` skill payloads in this session. I worked from the one-line rules in `rules.md`.\n\n- **build-the-lever:** the behaviour is covered by rerunnable `node:test` cases that use decoys and a mid-flight skim, instead of by hand inspection.\n- **unslop:** I kept the README and notification wording short and concrete.\n\nI can't say either skill changed a decision beyond that.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01ThreBzXX9EFx5szysmiav7","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
